  "lifeAreasTitle": "Life areas",
  "lifeAreasIntro": "Needs assessment under the German Federal Participation Act (Sec. 118 SGB IX) is structured around the nine life areas of the ICF component “Activities and Participation” (chapters d1–d9). Tap a life area to browse its ICF categories.",
  "guideBioPsychoSocial": "The biopsychosocial model",
  "guideBioPsychoSocialText": "The ICF is based on the biopsychosocial model of functioning and disability: a person's participation results from the interaction of their health condition (ICD diagnosis) with body functions and structures (b/s), activities and participation (d), and contextual factors — the environment (e) and personal factors. Disability is not an attribute of a person but the outcome of an interaction: the same impairment can lead to very different participation depending on environmental factors (assistive devices, barriers, attitudes). This is why a complete ICF description always documents facilitators and barriers as well.",
  "guideCodingRules": "Coding guidelines (summary)",
  "guideCodingRulesIntro": "The ICF (Annex 2) sets binding rules for coding. The most important ones in brief:",
  "guideCodingRule1": "Code only relevant information: code what is significant in the context of the health condition — no coding on stock.",
  "guideCodingRule2": "No inferences: functioning must not be inferred from a diagnosis or vice versa. Every code needs an explicit source of information (observation, interview, assessment, records).",
  "guideCodingRule3": "Codes are only complete with a qualifier: an ICF code without a qualifier has no meaning. At least the first qualifier must be given.",
  "guideCodingRule4": "Use 8 and 9 deliberately: “8 – not specified” means a problem exists but its extent is unknown; “9 – not applicable” means the category does not apply (e.g. d850 Remunerative employment for a toddler).",
  "guideCodingRule5": "Be as specific as possible: always choose the most precise applicable category (e.g. d4500 Walking short distances rather than d450 Walking) when the information allows.",
  "guideCodingRule6": "Environmental factors from the person's perspective: whether something is a barrier or a facilitator is judged from the perspective of the person being described.",
  "guidePerformanceCapacity": "Performance and capacity: for Activities and Participation (d), the ICF provides two qualifiers. The first describes performance — what a person does in their current environment, including assistive devices and personal assistance. The second describes capacity — the highest possible level in a standardised environment without assistance. The gap between the two shows what environmental adjustments achieve — and that gap is the starting point of needs assessment.",
  "guideIcfCy": "ICF-CY: children and youth",
  "guideIcfCyText": "For children and adolescents aged 0–18, the WHO published the ICF-CY (Children and Youth) in 2007. It extends the ICF with categories for developmental stages — such as play, language acquisition, or learning through imitation — and is the reference instrument in early intervention and participation planning for children. The WHO has since gradually merged the ICF-CY categories into the main ICF. No freely available German edition of the ICF-CY currently exists; the German translation was published as a book (Hollenweger/Kraus de Camargo, Hogrefe). This app contains the ICF reference version (BfArM, 2005); the ICF-CY-specific categories are not included.",
  "coreSetsTitle": "ICF Core Sets",
  "coreSetsIntro": "ICF Core Sets are scientifically agreed selections of the ICF categories most relevant to a health condition. They offer a quick entry point for documentation — but they do not replace individual needs assessment, because the decisive contextual factors are always individual.",
  "coreSetSource": "Source",
